---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const cards = [
  ['Decision','Rank planned contacts just before they are made. Only information that exists at that moment may be a feature.'],
  ['Records','One row is one record. The file has no client identifier, so records are never called distinct clients.'],
  ['Features','18 main columns. duration is excluded (unknown before the call) and campaign is excluded (its eligibility cannot be established); both are re-tested in sensitivity runs.'],
  ['Data roles','Development (80%, seed 42, stratified) for every choice, comparison (20%) scored once for frozen pipelines, and a chronological split (first 80% of rows / last 20%) for part 16.'],
  ['Search protocol','Default plus up to seven random candidates per model, shuffled 3-fold CV on development rows, scored on average precision. Candidate counts are measured, not assumed.'],
  ['Price list','Cost 1 per contact, value 8 per subscription: illustrative assumptions. Contributions are retrospective policy simulations, not value caused by contacting.'],
  ['Reporting','Average precision, AUC, log loss, Brier, ECE with counts, simulated contribution against baselines, paired bootstrap with simultaneous intervals.'],
  ['Synthetic track','Fruit lab: one controlled factor changed at a time; fixed random seed 20260930.'],
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Methods" alt={{ lang: 'he', href: `${import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '')}/he/series/classification/methods/` }}>
  <section class="section prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Methods at a glance</p>
    <h1>One protocol, visible assumptions</h1>
    <p class="lede">These choices make the comparisons interpretable. They do not turn one dataset into universal evidence.</p>
    <div class="methods-grid">{cards.map(([h,p]) => <article><h2>{h}</h2><p>{p}</p></article>)}</div>
    <h2 id="scope">What the benchmark can establish</h2>
    <p>It estimates how these concrete pipelines behave on a comparison sample (and, in part 16, on later records) of this dataset. The comparison split was looked at in earlier drafts of this series, so it is a controlled teaching benchmark, not a fresh test set. The benchmark can reveal sensitivity to the split, class rarity, objectives and thresholds. It cannot identify the causal effect of contacting people, prove that row order matches calendar time, or crown a model family for every tabular problem.</p>
    <h2 id="artifacts">Audit the run</h2>
    <p>Start with the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}series/classification/artifacts/protocol_manifest.json`}>protocol manifest</a> (splits, feature decisions, versions, seeds), the <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}series/classification/artifacts/feature_availability.csv`}>feature availability table</a>, then <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}series/classification/artifacts/leaderboard_selected.csv`}>every model's selected configuration and scores</a>, <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}series/classification/artifacts/leaderboard_candidates.csv`}>every candidate evaluated</a> and <a href={`${import.meta.env.BASE_URL}series/classification/artifacts/leaderboard_predictions.csv`}>comparison-split scores for all twelve models</a>. Preprocessing sits inside each fitted pipeline so encoders and scalers learn from training rows only. Tables and numbers in the articles are rendered from these files.</p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

